---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/diagram.css';
import ArchDiagram from '../../components/ArchDiagram.astro';
import { NODES, LINKS, INFO, FLOWS, BOXES } from '../../data/argocd-diagram';

const order = ['api', 'repo', 'ctrl', 'redis', 'dex', 'git', 'cluster', 'appset', 'notif', 'user', 'dev'];
---
<Base title="Argo CD architecture" description="An animated, interactive architecture diagram of Argo CD: hover each component to see what it does and how requests flow through it." progress>
  <div class="wrap cat">
    <a class="back reveal" href="/system-design/">← System Design</a>

    <header class="ad-head">
      <p class="kicker reveal blur" style="--i:0"><span class="dot"></span> System design · GitOps</p>
      <h1 class="reveal-title" aria-label="Argo CD architecture"><span style="--i:1">Argo CD</span><span style="--i:2">architecture</span></h1>
      <p class="cat-desc reveal blur" style="--i:3">Watch a request move through Argo CD. Pick a flow, step through it, and hover any component to see what it does and how requests flow through it.</p>
    </header>

    <ArchDiagram nodes={NODES} links={LINKS} info={INFO} flows={FLOWS} boxes={BOXES} label="Argo CD architecture diagram" />

    <div class="prose ad-explain">
      <h2>The idea in one minute</h2>
      <p>Argo CD is a GitOps continuous-delivery tool for Kubernetes. You describe what should run in Git, and Argo CD keeps the cluster matching it. Nothing pushes into the cluster from a pipeline: Argo CD pulls from Git and reconciles.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Desired state</strong> lives in Git, as plain YAML, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays.</li>
        <li><strong>Live state</strong> is what is actually running in the target cluster.</li>
        <li><strong>The reconcile loop:</strong> the Application Controller compares the two. Equal means Synced. Different means OutOfSync, and Argo CD can sync automatically.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Core concepts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Application</strong> — a custom resource linking a source (repo, path, revision) to a destination (cluster, namespace).</li>
        <li><strong>AppProject</strong> — groups applications and restricts which repos, clusters and namespaces they may use.</li>
        <li><strong>Sync status and health</strong> — Synced or OutOfSync for the comparison; Healthy, Progressing, Degraded, Suspended, Missing or Unknown for the running resources.</li>
        <li><strong>Sync policy</strong> — manual or automated. Automated sync can also <code>prune</code> resources deleted from Git and <code>selfHeal</code> manual changes in the cluster.</li>
        <li><strong>Hooks and waves</strong> — PreSync, Sync, PostSync and SyncFail hooks, and the <code>argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave</code> annotation to order resources.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where the configuration lives</h2>
      <p>Argo CD is configured through a few resources in its namespace: <code>argocd-cm</code> for the URL, SSO and general settings, <code>argocd-rbac-cm</code> for roles and group mapping, and <code>argocd-secret</code> for secrets such as the OIDC client secret.</p>
      <p>See how this looks in practice: <a href="/how-to/argocd-integration/">ArgoCD Integration</a>, <a href="/how-to/argocd-readonly-user/">Read-Only User and SSO</a>, and <a href="/how-to/argocd-upgrade-backup/">Upgrade &amp; Backup</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="ad-ref">
      <h2 class="reveal">Components at a glance</h2>
      <div class="ad-ref-grid">
        {order.map((id, i) => (
          <div class="ad-ref-card reveal" style={`--i:${i % 3}`}>
            <p class="k">{INFO[id].tag}</p>
            <h3>{INFO[id].name}</h3>
            <p>{INFO[id].does}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

</Base>
